>
  <p>PLA's reputation as a beginner material undersells it. The fastest FDM printers in the world — Bambu X1C, Voron 2.4 with input shaping, tuned CoreXY machines — all run PLA as their primary material. The speed ceiling for PLA is higher than for PETG, ASA, or ABS because it requires aggressive active cooling, which is also what lets it print fast. If your machine is tuned, PLA will show you what it can actually do.</p>

  <p>The practical limitations are real but specific. PLA fails in heat — leave a PLA part on a car dashboard in summer and it will warp. It fails in UV exposure over time — outdoor fixtures printed in PLA will become brittle and discolored within months. And it fails under sudden impact loads better than it fails under sustained stress. Know these limits and PLA is excellent. Ignore them and you'll blame the printer for a material choice problem.</p>

  <p>On brand choice — the difference between top-tier and budget PLA shows up in dimensional accuracy and batch consistency, not print quality on a good day. Budget PLA from a reliable batch prints just as well as Prusament. The issue is reliability across batches and over time. For one-off prints, buy cheap. For anything where fit and finish matter across multiple prints, spend the extra money on Prusament or Bambu.</p>
</FilamentLayout>
